{"name":"Mizu","hex":"#70c1e0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Godai (Japanese philosophy)","blocks":[{"text":"Godai (五大; lit. \"five – great, large, physical, form\") are the five elements in Japanese Buddhist thought: earth (chi), water (sui), fire (ka), wind (fu), and void (ku). Its origins are from the Indian Buddhist concept of Mahābhūta, disseminated and influenced by Chinese traditions before being absorbed, influenced, and refined into and by Japanese tradition, culture, and indigenous folk religions."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godai_(Japanese_philosophy)","wikidata":"Q3109675"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/It-%E7%A9%BA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/22/It-%E7%A9%BA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Void (Aether)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIt-%E7%A9%BA.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg/500px-T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg/1280px-T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg/500px-T%C5%8Dky%C5%8D_j%C5%ABnikagetsu%2C_Yanaka_no_y%C5%ABbae_by_Kawase_Hasui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kawase Hasui's \"Evening Glow at Yanaka\"